Serving static resources in a way that takes advantage of browser caching across Shiny apps is the best thing I could ask for.  This is good not only for app start-up response time for the user, but also affects the rate at which (and perhaps the extent to which (?)) search engine crawlers will crawl your site if you have several Shiny apps.

A somewhat-distant secondary feature that comes to mind, involves Shiny app user interfaces that are dependent on URL parameters.  In such apps, one can have the UI (such as returned by ui.R) be a function instead of an object. Having the UI be a function gives you the option to have the initial page returned to the client (or a search engine crawler) be more fully-formed, as opposed to needing to interact with your app's server over the websocket to flesh out the portions of the UI corresponding to textOutputs and uiOutputs.  I mention this because it has to do with an issue that I've not yet fully grokked:  how dynamically-generated Shiny UIs (e.g. apps that make extensive use of uiOutput() and textOutput(), for example) appear to search engine spiders.  The pages of such Shiny apps can appear very sparse to at least some search engine crawlers.  (Note this is the same problem that AngularJS apps have, for example.)  It seems as if Google's bot has the *ability* to interact with a Shiny app and get the complete, initial picture of a page, but, in practice, it seems to stop short of doing it completely.

One solution is to use a service like http://prerender.io/ .  However, it has crossed my mind that there might be a better solution whereby Shiny could initially return that more fully-formed page, rather than have the initial values for uiOutput() and textOutput() get filled in after the initial page is returned to the client. This might also improve the user-perceived app start-up time a little bit. Perhaps that kind of "prerender" thing would be implemented within Shiny itself though, rather than Shiny Server.

